WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.160 --> 00:00:01.560
Countless visitors

00:00:01.560 --> 00:00:04.430
flock to New York City's
Madison Square Park

00:00:04.430 --> 00:00:06.850
to gaze upon and take selfies with

00:00:06.850 --> 00:00:09.223
some of the world's most famous buildings.

00:00:10.480 --> 00:00:12.620
But even native New
Yorkers probably missed

00:00:12.620 --> 00:00:15.610
one of the most interesting
stories in the city's skyline,

00:00:15.610 --> 00:00:19.653
located here, and that's because
most of it doesn't exist.

00:00:25.080 --> 00:00:27.880
During the major economic
expansion of New York City,

00:00:27.880 --> 00:00:30.820
in the late 19th and early 20th centuries,

00:00:30.820 --> 00:00:34.843
demand and prices for prime
real estate in Manhattan soared.

00:00:35.980 --> 00:00:37.760
Thanks to advancements in construction

00:00:37.760 --> 00:00:39.230
and elevator technology,

00:00:39.230 --> 00:00:41.560
developers could suddenly build vertically

00:00:41.560 --> 00:00:43.770
to increase usable space,

00:00:43.770 --> 00:00:46.710
essentially creating land in the sky.

00:00:46.710 --> 00:00:49.370
Skyscrapers, as these
mega-projects were called,

00:00:49.370 --> 00:00:52.300
started popping up in the
priciest parts of the city,

00:00:52.300 --> 00:00:53.439
often named after the companies

00:00:53.439 --> 00:00:55.280
that funded their construction

00:00:55.280 --> 00:00:57.263
in a clever form of brand marketing.

00:00:59.380 --> 00:01:01.450
In the latter half of the roaring 1920s,

00:01:01.450 --> 00:01:03.750
the Metropolitan Life Insurance Company

00:01:03.750 --> 00:01:05.950
wanted to construct a new skyscraper

00:01:05.950 --> 00:01:07.800
to house its growing staff,

00:01:07.800 --> 00:01:11.463
alongside its existing landmark
headquarters on 24th Street.

00:01:12.400 --> 00:01:13.780
The ambitious plan called for

00:01:13.780 --> 00:01:16.350
the tallest building in
the world at the time,

00:01:16.350 --> 00:01:18.180
around 100 stories,

00:01:18.180 --> 00:01:20.500
incorporating the distinctive setback look

00:01:20.500 --> 00:01:21.990
that was required by New York City's

00:01:21.990 --> 00:01:23.793
recently adopted zoning code.

00:01:25.260 --> 00:01:27.510
There was one problem, however,

00:01:27.510 --> 00:01:29.680
during the same week of
the plans being announced

00:01:29.680 --> 00:01:31.930
the U.S. stock market had collapsed,

00:01:31.930 --> 00:01:33.870
signaling the end of the roaring '20s,

00:01:33.870 --> 00:01:35.970
and the beginning of the Great Depression.

00:01:37.200 --> 00:01:39.900
With funding scaled back the grand design,

00:01:39.900 --> 00:01:42.830
much like the decade of excess
in which it was conceived,

00:01:42.830 --> 00:01:44.540
was cut off.

00:01:44.540 --> 00:01:46.660
Eventually topping out at
barely a 1/3 of the height

00:01:46.660 --> 00:01:49.680
of the previous design,
leaving behind a stump

00:01:49.680 --> 00:01:52.693
that some might say is a
monument to economic hubris.

00:01:54.370 --> 00:01:56.890
Fast forward to today
and New York City skyline

00:01:56.890 --> 00:01:58.530
is again dotted with impressive

00:01:58.530 --> 00:02:00.550
and record-breaking skyscrapers.

00:02:00.550 --> 00:02:04.820
Many nearing completion, as
the global economy sputters.

00:02:04.820 --> 00:02:08.040
Some economists insist the
timing of these skyscraper booms

00:02:08.040 --> 00:02:11.280
and economic downturns is
more than just a coincidence.

00:02:11.280 --> 00:02:12.760
In fact, some have called it.

00:02:12.760 --> 00:02:14.860
The skyscraper curse.

00:02:14.860 --> 00:02:17.100
This is Mark Thornton, who, in 2007,

00:02:17.100 --> 00:02:20.430
I applied an economic model
called the skyscraper index

00:02:20.430 --> 00:02:21.790
to suggest that the construction

00:02:21.790 --> 00:02:25.780
of the world's tallest building,
the Burj Khalifa in Dubai,

00:02:25.780 --> 00:02:28.880
meant that a global
financial crisis was coming.

00:02:28.880 --> 00:02:30.773
Which it did a year or so later.

00:02:31.910 --> 00:02:35.630
The model he used is based
on an idea proposed in 1999

00:02:35.630 --> 00:02:38.410
by a real estate analyst
named Andrew Lawrence.

00:02:38.410 --> 00:02:40.900
He looked at all the
records setting skyscrapers

00:02:40.900 --> 00:02:44.100
beginning with the Singer
Building in New York City

00:02:44.100 --> 00:02:46.740
which correlated with the Panic of 1907,

00:02:46.740 --> 00:02:49.030
and then you go to the late 1920s

00:02:49.030 --> 00:02:50.217
and you see the Chrysler Building

00:02:50.217 --> 00:02:53.870
and the Empire State Building
setting records consecutively.

00:02:53.870 --> 00:02:56.010
And then, of course, the crash of '29.

00:02:56.010 --> 00:02:59.430
And then you fast forward
until you get to 1970,

00:02:59.430 --> 00:03:02.790
when World Trade Tower
one, World Trade Tower two,

00:03:02.790 --> 00:03:05.720
and the Sears building
all set new records.

00:03:05.720 --> 00:03:06.553
And then, of course,

00:03:06.553 --> 00:03:10.910
we had the long stagflation of the 1970s.

00:03:10.910 --> 00:03:13.140
Fast forward from that you get

00:03:13.140 --> 00:03:17.150
new record-setting
skyscrapers in the late 1990s,

00:03:17.150 --> 00:03:20.240
and early 2000s, and of course,
right in the middle of that

00:03:20.240 --> 00:03:22.883
was the tech stock bubble bust.

00:03:25.150 --> 00:03:27.830
The correlations holding up very well.

00:03:27.830 --> 00:03:29.550
And some see those warning signs

00:03:29.550 --> 00:03:31.240
on the rise again.

00:03:31.240 --> 00:03:33.400
Entrepreneurs did great.

00:03:33.400 --> 00:03:36.640
Everybody was making money for so long.

00:03:36.640 --> 00:03:38.960
But we're gonna see is
that there's going to be

00:03:38.960 --> 00:03:43.450
this cluster of areas where
companies have to cut back,

00:03:43.450 --> 00:03:47.760
retrench, go bankrupt, foreclose.

00:03:47.760 --> 00:03:49.490
We're certainly not looking forward to it

00:03:49.490 --> 00:03:52.203
in the sense of being a joyful event.

00:03:53.270 --> 00:03:54.590
But the skyscraper index

00:03:54.590 --> 00:03:56.810
is, to put it lightly, controversial.

00:03:56.810 --> 00:04:01.810
No offense to you but skyscraper curse,

00:04:02.150 --> 00:04:03.940
I really wish we would put this to bed.

00:04:03.940 --> 00:04:04.840
This is Jason Barr,

00:04:04.840 --> 00:04:06.680
an economist and author specializes

00:04:06.680 --> 00:04:08.660
in what he calls skynomics,

00:04:08.660 --> 00:04:10.870
and he co authored an empirical analysis

00:04:10.870 --> 00:04:13.440
of the skyscraper index and found that.

00:04:13.440 --> 00:04:17.690
It's an example of what I
call broken clockism. Okay?

00:04:17.690 --> 00:04:22.550
If you're looking to
pair a skyscraper boom

00:04:22.550 --> 00:04:25.640
with a downturn it's easy

00:04:25.640 --> 00:04:28.040
because the downturns are
happening all the time.

00:04:28.040 --> 00:04:29.277
You can just say, "Oh, here's the peak

00:04:29.277 --> 00:04:31.847
"of the skyscraper cycle. Oh,
here's the business cycle.

00:04:31.847 --> 00:04:34.030
"Oh, yeah, they must be
connected in some way."

00:04:34.030 --> 00:04:36.070
Although Barr's research
called into question

00:04:36.070 --> 00:04:39.480
the forecasting potential of
record-breaking skyscrapers,

00:04:39.480 --> 00:04:42.010
it did confirm that
skyscraper construction waves

00:04:42.010 --> 00:04:45.090
typically rise when economic
conditions are favorable,

00:04:45.090 --> 00:04:47.140
sometimes to the point of a bubble,

00:04:47.140 --> 00:04:50.020
and then slow is the project's
make less financial sense

00:04:50.020 --> 00:04:51.360
and funding dries up,

00:04:51.360 --> 00:04:54.640
much like the case of the
Metropolitan Life North Building.

00:04:54.640 --> 00:04:55.930
It's kind of a herding mentality.

00:04:55.930 --> 00:04:59.230
When the getting's good, everybody,

00:04:59.230 --> 00:05:01.570
all these developers they rush
to get their buildings online

00:05:01.570 --> 00:05:04.570
as soon as possible, and
then you have a glut.

00:05:04.570 --> 00:05:07.170
You can conceive of a project
based on the economics today

00:05:07.170 --> 00:05:10.440
and then when it comes online,
four or five years from now,

00:05:10.440 --> 00:05:12.270
anything could happen.

00:05:12.270 --> 00:05:13.940
In other words, skyscrapers simply take

00:05:13.940 --> 00:05:16.830
a long time to build and
some end up being completed

00:05:16.830 --> 00:05:19.480
after the next downturn has already begun.

00:05:19.480 --> 00:05:23.420
This timing, if anything, is
more like a lagging indicator,

00:05:23.420 --> 00:05:25.260
then a leading one.

00:05:25.260 --> 00:05:27.760
I guess, I just wish the
skyscraper curse thing,

00:05:28.740 --> 00:05:30.040
that people would stop using it

00:05:30.040 --> 00:05:34.960
because there's zero evidence
that it actually exists.

00:05:34.960 --> 00:05:35.793
But what makes something

00:05:35.793 --> 00:05:39.163
like the skyscraper index so
alluring in the first place?

00:05:40.090 --> 00:05:42.960
For one, unlike many
other economic indicators

00:05:42.960 --> 00:05:47.260
like consumer price indices,
unemployment rates, or GDP.

00:05:47.260 --> 00:05:49.400
Skyscrapers are so visible.

00:05:49.400 --> 00:05:52.650
They are super visible.
They're ultra visible.

00:05:52.650 --> 00:05:56.050
I mean, the skyline is
very notion and identity

00:05:56.050 --> 00:05:57.620
of the city in a lot of ways.

00:05:57.620 --> 00:06:01.990
Skyscrapers are a marvel of technology.

00:06:01.990 --> 00:06:05.880
When you think about
symbols of civilization,

00:06:05.880 --> 00:06:09.010
a skyscraper is one of them.

00:06:09.010 --> 00:06:11.290
But ultimately skyscrapers
are simply products

00:06:11.290 --> 00:06:12.760
of our economic system,

00:06:12.760 --> 00:06:15.867
or as skyscraper historian
Carol Willis has called them,

00:06:15.867 --> 00:06:18.850
"the ultimate architecture of capitalism."

00:06:18.850 --> 00:06:20.950
Fundamentally, skyscrapers are a part

00:06:20.950 --> 00:06:24.060
of the larger story of human urbanization.

00:06:24.060 --> 00:06:26.180
That all the forces that draws to cities,

00:06:26.180 --> 00:06:29.050
create this need to build
offices and high rise buildings

00:06:29.050 --> 00:06:31.120
that accommodate all of
these people who wanna

00:06:31.120 --> 00:06:33.070
be at the same place at the same time.

00:06:33.070 --> 00:06:35.080
This demand for access to urban space

00:06:35.080 --> 00:06:39.060
has fueled a global skyscraper
boom during the past decade.

00:06:39.060 --> 00:06:41.740
And according to the
Skyscraper Center database,

00:06:41.740 --> 00:06:44.530
over 130 super-tall construction projects

00:06:44.530 --> 00:06:47.320
are currently underway worldwide.

00:06:47.320 --> 00:06:49.710
But as social distancing and remote work

00:06:49.710 --> 00:06:53.130
call into question the
demand for dense urban life,

00:06:53.130 --> 00:06:56.023
skyscrapers may be taking
on an entirely new meaning.

00:06:57.080 --> 00:06:59.290
After all, if a full busy skyscraper

00:06:59.290 --> 00:07:01.330
can represent power and prosperity,

00:07:01.330 --> 00:07:03.620
an unfinished or empty one can stand in

00:07:03.620 --> 00:07:05.013
for economic transition.

00:07:06.360 --> 00:07:11.360
Take Detroit, economic crisis
of the 1960s and 1970s.

00:07:12.980 --> 00:07:16.923
We saw for a long time skyscrapers
with very little value.

00:07:18.520 --> 00:07:21.760
I don't think there's any indication, yet,

00:07:21.760 --> 00:07:24.610
that we can expect this on some mass scale

00:07:24.610 --> 00:07:28.910
across the United States
but at the same time,

00:07:28.910 --> 00:07:31.830
the ravages of COVID-19
are totally unchecked.

00:07:31.830 --> 00:07:34.340
So it's very hard to predict

00:07:34.340 --> 00:07:37.450
the full economic consequences
of what's happening.

00:07:37.450 --> 00:07:39.920
As always, the future remains to be seen.

00:07:39.920 --> 00:07:42.190
But one thing does seem likely,

00:07:42.190 --> 00:07:44.430
if the skyscraper boom does come to an end

00:07:44.430 --> 00:07:47.760
as the economy shifts, don't
be surprised if some say

00:07:47.760 --> 00:07:49.483
the curse may be striking again.

